(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_thesolidstate=[{id:"neet-2023-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Voids in close packing",q:"A compound is formed by two elements A and B. The element B forms cubic close packed structure and atoms of A occupy 1/3 of tetrahedral voids. If the formula of the compound is AxBy, then the value of x + y is in option",o:["2","5","4","3"],c:1,s:`Let the number of B atoms be N.
A ccp lattice of N atoms has 2N tetrahedral voids.
A fills 1/3 of them, so A atoms = (1)/(3) \xD7 2N = (2N)/(3).
Ratio A : B = (2N/3) : N = 2 : 3, so the formula is A\u2082B\u2083.
x = 2, y = 3, so x + y = 5.`},{id:"neet-2023-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Voids in close packing",q:"What fraction of one edge centred octahedral void lies in one unit cell of fcc?",o:["(1)/(12)","(1)/(2)","(1)/(3)","(1)/(4)"],c:3,s:`An fcc unit cell has four octahedral voids in all.
They are at the edge centres and the body centre.
An edge-centre void is shared by four unit cells, so each cell holds 1/4 of it.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q057",pn:57,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Point defects in solids",q:`Match List - I with List - II :
List-I (Defects): (a) Frenkel defect (b) Schottky defect (c) Vacancy defect (d) Interstitial defect
List-II (shown by): (i) non-ionic solids and density of the solid decreases (ii) non-ionic solids and density of the solid increases (iii) ionic solids and density of the solid decreases (iv) ionic solids and density of the solid remains constant
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (ii)","(a) - (i), (b) - (ii), (c) - (iii), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (i), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)"],c:0,s:`Schottky and Frenkel defects occur in ionic solids.
Schottky lowers the density; Frenkel keeps it constant.
Vacancy and interstitial defects occur in non-ionic solids.
Vacancy lowers the density; interstitial raises it.
So (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii).`},{id:"neet-2022-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Point defects",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : In a particular point defect, an ionic solid is electrically neutral, even if few of its cations are missing from its unit cells.
Reason (R) : In an ionic solid, Frenkel defect arises due to dislocation of cation from its lattice site to interstitial site, maintaining overall electrical neutrality.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],c:0,s:`The assertion describes a Schottky-type or impurity defect where ions are missing and the solid stays neutral.
The reason correctly describes the Frenkel defect, where a cation moves to an interstitial site and no ion leaves the crystal.
Both are true, but the reason does not explain the assertion.`},{id:"neet-2022-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Density of unit cell",q:"Copper crystallises in fcc unit cell with cell edge length of 3.608 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 cm. The density of copper is 8.92 g cm\u207B\xB3. Calculate the atomic mass of copper.",o:["31.55 u","60 u","65 u","63.1 u"],c:3,s:`Density d = ZM/(N_A a\xB3), so M = d N_A a\xB3/Z.
For fcc, Z = 4. N_A = 6.023 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3, a = 3.608 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 cm.
a\xB3 = 46.97 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2074 cm\xB3.
M = (8.92 \xD7 6.023 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3 \xD7 46.97 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2074)/(4) \u2248 63.1 u.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q099",pn:99,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Non-stoichiometric defects",q:"What fraction of Fe exists as Fe(III) in Fe\u2080.\u2089\u2086O ? (Consider Fe\u2080.\u2089\u2086O to be made up of Fe(II) and Fe(III) only)",o:["1/20","1/12","0.08","1/16"],c:1,s:`Write Fe\u2080.\u2089\u2086O as Fe\u2089\u2086O\u2081\u2080\u2080.
Let x Fe atoms be Fe\xB2\u207A, so (96 - x) are Fe\xB3\u207A.
Total positive charge = total negative charge: 2x + 3(96 - x) = 200.
288 - x = 200, so x = 88.
Fe\xB3\u207A = 96 - 88 = 8.
Fraction of Fe\xB3\u207A = 8/96 = 1/12.`},{id:"neet-2021-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Voids in close packing",q:"Right option for the number of tetrahedral and octahedral voids in hexagonal primitive unit cell are :",o:["12, 6","8, 4","6, 12","2, 1"],c:0,s:`N close-packed atoms give N octahedral voids and 2N tetrahedral voids.
A hexagonal primitive unit cell has 6 atoms.
Octahedral voids = 6, tetrahedral voids = 12.
Order asked is tetrahedral, octahedral: 12, 6.`},{id:"neet-2021-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Bravais lattices",q:"The correct option for the number of body centred unit cells in all 14 types of Bravais lattice unit cells is :",o:["3","7","5","2"],c:0,s:`Body-centred lattices occur in the cubic, tetragonal and orthorhombic systems.
So there are 3 body-centred unit cells.`}]})();
